Given that three faucets fill a 100-gallon tub in 6 minutes, the rate of the faucets can be calculated as follows: \[
\text{Rate of three faucets} = \frac{100 \text{ gallons}}{6 \text{ minutes}}
\] The rate per faucet is: \[
\text{Rate per faucet} = \frac{100 \text{ gallons} / 3}{6 \text{ minutes}} = \frac{100}{18} \text{ gallons per minute} = \frac{50}{9} \text{ gallons per minute}
\] For six faucets, the combined rate would be: \[
\text{Rate of six faucets} = 6 \times \frac{50}{9} = \frac{300}{9} = \frac{100}{3} \text{ gallons per minute}
\] To find out how long it takes for six faucets to fill a 25-gallon tub, we can use the formula: \[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Volume}}{\text{Rate}} = \frac{25 \text{ gallons}}{\frac{100}{3} \text{ gallons per minute}} = \frac{25 \times 3}{100} \text{ minutes} = \frac{75}{100} \text{ minutes} = 0.75 \text{ minutes}
\] Since 1 minute is equal to 60 seconds, the time in seconds is: \[
0.75 \text{ minutes} \times 60 \text{ seconds per minute} = 45 \text{ seconds}
\] So the answer is 45 seconds. </think> <answer>45</think>

